= Conclusions <sec:conclusions>

This thesis reviewed the different Earth orbits and their use-cases and examined
the considerations relevant to the use of commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) parts
in small satellite systems. The space environment imposes constraints, most
notably vacuum, launch loads and radiation, that differ fundamentally from the
terrestrial use-cases COTS parts are designed for.

Two representative environmental effects were analysed in more detail.
Atmospheric drag was shown to both limit the lifetime of satellites in low earth
orbit and, when deliberately controlled, to serve as a propellant-free means of
orbit and constellation control. Radiation exposure was identified as the most
damaging factor for human spaceflight, and passive as well as active shielding
strategies were discussed together with their respective limitations.

